hairston@henry.ece.cmu.edu (David Hairston) writes:

>let's start with the premise:
>you should be able to drop into a pool of water and escape death
>under some circumstances, but at what price? what is it worth?
>what works???

No, I don't think anyone is suggesting that you should be able to escape
death AFTER falling in the water (though that might be something else
worth considering...like if you are polyd to an eel).  The idea is that
the act of entering water should be confirmed, the same way that an attempt
to hit a monster of your alignment is confirmed (if the option is set).

I suggest (as someone else has), that

1. One might sometimes actually want to attack a friendly monster
2. The game will confirm that, if you want, to help avoid mistakes
3. One is much less likely to actually want to enter water
4. If #2 is available, why not allow confirmation for entering water?

It's interesting how differently and alike we think, we two Davids:

When we were talking about wishing, I felt that the simple "What do you
wish for?" prompt was excellent, since it was so deliciously intimidating!
David H, on the other hand, felt that the player should not be penalized
for lousy spelling or inadequate parsing, so he wanted a menu tree.

Now we're dealing with a very similar issue: should the player be penalized
for his lack of real-life dexterity on the keyboard?  I think not -- at least
it's not in the spirit of nethack, a non-real-time strategy thinking game.

Interesting...I just had a childhood memory of my first game of dungeons
and dragons.  We rolled our characters, got all set and entered the
dungeon.  Not ten steps in, the path bifurcated.  We all chose to stick
together and take the right fork.  FOOM!  The DM gleefully informed us that
we had walked into a trap that made spikes fly out from the walls, killing
everyone.  That was also my LAST game of d&d.

Don't get me wrong -- I have seen a lot of posts here which I would classify
as "sour grapes" postings.  Someone has just lost a great game, and so
proposes that the specific situation which caused his death should be
removed from the game.  Okay, that's not reasonable.

But it seems inconsistent to suddenly require 'video game' skills in nethack.
Not wrong...not unplayable, but inconsistent, and whether is was meant to
be a dirty trick or not, it feels like one..and not just the first time!


